      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes a lot of sense, thanks for clarifying the science behind it. I'll definitely look into soil testing to see if I'm dealing with leaching or something else.\n\nI'm particularly interested in the microbial side of things, though. You mentioned that microorganisms influence nitrogen cycling, and since I'm doing organic farming, I rely heavily on those \"invisible\" biological processes to do the heavy lifting.\n\nWhen I walk through my beds, I sometimes get this really strange impression that things are constantly shifting and moving around just below the surface, even when I haven't been out there tilling. It’s almost like there’s a whole hidden world down there that's interacting in ways I just can't see. What are the actual biological indicators that tell me the microbial population is truly active and working *for* my plants, rather than just being a collection of organisms that aren't actually helping the nutrient cycle?",
